  "segment": "Choosing a financial advisor",
  "h1": "Robo-Advisor vs Financial Advisor: Which One Fits You?",
  "introText": "Pick a robo-advisor if you want low-cost automated investing for a defined goal and accept little human contact; pick a human adviser if you need advice beyond investing or want a person to talk to. In the guides we publish here, we look at the specific legal duties and disclosure filings behind financial advice rather than sales marketing. At ModernWallet, we show the math, name what moves it, and explain the tradeoff.\n\nBoth models can provide ongoing portfolio management, but their operational methods and service scopes differ. An automated digital investment advisory program collects your goals and risk tolerance through an online questionnaire, then builds and manages a portfolio. A human investment adviser provides ongoing investment advice and monitoring, and can deliver planning services alongside securities management.",
  "comparisonTable": {
    "rows": [
      {
        "dimension": "Primary service model",
        "a": "Automated digital program creating and managing an investment portfolio",
        "b": "Ongoing advice, portfolio monitoring, and optional broad financial planning"
      },
