        "a": "Commonly constructed using exchange-traded funds (ETFs)",
        "b": "Securities"
      }
    ]
  },
  "verdict": "A robo-advisor fits a reader who wants low-cost automated investing for a defined goal and accepts little human contact; a human adviser fits a reader who needs advice beyond investing or wants a person to call. If the human is a registered investment adviser, the duty of care and loyalty is the same as a robo-advisor's; if the person is a broker or dual registrant, a different standard may apply, shown in Form CRS. This outcome changes if an automated platform provides a hybrid human adviser at your balance level, or if the human is broker-only. Read both firms' relationship summaries and Form ADV Part 2 disclosures to compare all-in costs and human access before opening an account.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Robo-Advisor or Financial Advisor: Which Fits Your Situation",
      "content": "Pick a robo-advisor if you want low-cost automated investing for a defined goal and accept little human contact; pick a human adviser if you need advice beyond investing or want a person to talk to. Your choice between a robo-advisor and a traditional human advisor depends on the complexity of your financial profile and your preferences for personal support. If your goal is automated investing for a defined goal, an automated platform builds and manages a portfolio. If you require advice across taxes, estate planning, or complex situations, a human advisor can supply planning services.\n\nAccording to the [SEC Investor Bulletin on Robo-Advisers](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-45) published by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Office of Investor Education and Advocacy (OIEA), a robo-advisor is an automated digital investment advisory program. These platforms collect your financial goals, investment time horizon, household income, other assets, and risk tolerance through an online questionnaire. The software then uses that collected information to build and manage an investment portfolio for you. Robo-advisors often present lower costs and fees and lower account minimums than traditional advisory programs.\n\nHuman investment advisers operate differently during the initial evaluation process. As detailed by [Investor.gov on Investment Advisers](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/investment-advisers), an investment adviser is a firm or person that provides investment advice about securities for compensation. These professionals typically provide ongoing advice about buying, selling, or holding investments, monitor performance, and may give planning services. Unlike an automated program that relies on an online questionnaire, a traditional adviser can hold a conversation to gather your needs. Understanding [what a financial advisor is worth](/guides/financial-advisor-worth-it/) starts with matching these specific service capabilities to your financial needs."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Side-by-Side Comparison of Advisory Models",
      "content": "Evaluating automated programs and human advisors side by side clarifies their operational differences. Both categories manage investment securities for individual clients, yet their workflows, fee mechanisms, and client obligations differ. The following table contrasts a robo-advisor with a human investment adviser who is a registered investment adviser.\n\n| Feature | Robo-Advisor | Human Registered Investment Adviser |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Primary focus | Automated digital portfolio management | Ongoing portfolio oversight and comprehensive planning |\n| Intake procedure | Structured online questionnaire | Direct conversation to gather your needs |\n| Portfolio building blocks | Frequently exchange-traded funds (ETFs) | Securities |\n| Human communication | Technical help only, tiered balance access, or hybrid | Direct conversation with a person |\n| Legal standard | Fiduciary duty of care and loyalty | Fiduciary duty of care and loyalty |\n| Disciplinary and background checks | Form ADV Part 1 and Form CRS | Form ADV Part 1, Part 2B supplements, and Form CRS |\n| Client responsibility | Must manually update profile details online | Negotiated by contract |\n\nSelecting between these formats requires balancing automated simplicity against customized human guidance. You can track your total accumulated wealth as you evaluate these structures by using our [net worth calculator](/net-worth/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Robo-Advisors and Financial Advisors Cost You",
      "content": "Comparing costs between automated platforms and personal advisers requires examining both fee structures and auxiliary account expenses. Most traditional investment advisers charge an asset-based fee calculated as a percentage of the total assets held in your account. As noted in the [Investor.gov Relationship Summary (Form CRS)](https://www.investor.gov/CRS) guidance, advisers are generally focused on your portfolio over time and typically paid an ongoing asset-based fee on total account value.\n\nRobo-advisors utilize diverse pricing frameworks, ranging from asset-based percentage fees to recurring subscriptions. The [SEC Investor Bulletin on Subscription-Based Advisory Fees](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/subscription-based-advisory-fees) notes that subscription pricing entails a set recurring fee, typically billed monthly. For smaller robo-adviser accounts, that fee might be $3, $5, or $10 per month for investment management. Many automated firms use these fixed monthly fees to market advisory services to newer investors with smaller account balances. \n\nAdvisory management charges do not encompass all investment expenses. Both human advisers and robo-advisers invest client money into underlying funds that carry internal operational expenses. The SEC bulletin on robo-advisers notes that many digital programs build portfolios using exchange-traded funds (ETFs), which deduct ongoing management expenses from fund performance. Similarly, clients may pay other fees such as brokerage, custody, and fund expenses. For detailed comparisons of conventional percentage models and fixed advisory retainers, read our comparison of [flat-fee vs AUM advisors](/compare/flat-fee-vs-aum-based-financial-advisors/), or explore specific automated choices in our guide to the [best robo-advisors](/roundup/best-robo-advisors/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How Much Human Contact You Get with Each Option",
      "content": "The level of personal contact varies widely across advisory options and should dictate which path you select. When working with a traditional human financial advisor, direct personal dialogue serves as the foundation of the relationship. You talk directly with a person who gathers your needs and provides ongoing advice. \n\nRobo-advisory platforms deliver varying levels of human communication. The SEC bulletin on robo-advisers explains that some digital programs offer access to an investment professional, often described as a hybrid or bionic model. Other automated platforms restrict human contact entirely to technical support staff. Furthermore, some automated firms condition access to human professionals on maintaining a minimum account size, leaving smaller account holders with purely digital interfaces.\n\nUnlike traditional advisory relationships, digital programs typically lack an initial conversation with a person to assess your personal needs. The SEC bulletin points out that robo-adviser recommendations are limited by the information requested, and the burden to update your profile data falls squarely on you. If your information changes, the platform cannot incorporate those developments until you update your profile."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Duty of Care Depends on How Your Advisor Is Registered",
      "content": "The legal standard of conduct governing your investment professional depends entirely on their regulatory registration. Many investors assume every financial professional operates under the same legal obligations, but regulatory classifications establish distinct legal duties. In a public statement on [Regulation Best Interest and the Investment Adviser Fiduciary Duty](https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/speeches-statements/clayton-regulation-best-interest-investment-adviser-fiduciary-duty), SEC Chairman Clayton explained that the investment adviser fiduciary duty is comprised of a duty of care and a duty of loyalty. This fiduciary framework applies to registered investment advisers, requiring them to act in the best interest of their clients and avoid placing their own financial interests ahead of the client's interests.\n\nRobo-advisors registered with the SEC operate as registered investment advisers, which means they owe clients an adviser fiduciary duty of care and loyalty. Similarly, human professionals who operate as registered investment advisers are held to this same fiduciary standard. However, many individual financial professionals do not operate solely as investment advisers. Some professionals are registered representatives of broker-dealers, while others are dual registrants licensed as both brokers and investment advisers. For broker-dealers, Regulation Best Interest establishes the governing standard of conduct, requiring brokers to act in the best interest of retail customers when making investment recommendations.\n\nDetermining which standard applies to your advisory relationship requires reviewing regulatory disclosure documents. According to the [SEC Investor Bulletin on Form ADV](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-71), retail investors should review Form ADV Part 2 and the Form CRS relationship summary. Form ADV Part 2A serves as an adviser's primary narrative brochure, detailing advisory business practices, fee schedules, conflict points, and disciplinary history across 18 standardized items. Form CRS explicitly states whether a firm is an investment adviser, a broker-dealer, or a dual registrant. You can verify firm registrations and review public filings on the SEC [Investment Adviser Public Disclosure (IAPD)](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov) database. Understanding [what a fiduciary is](/guides/what-is-a-fiduciary/) provides essential context when evaluating these legal relationships."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who Each Option Is Not For and What Would Change the Answer",
      "content": "A robo-advisor is not suitable for individuals who need tax or estate planning, or direct phone access to an adviser. \n\nSpecific operational changes can flip this general advisory recommendation. If an automated robo-advisory platform offers a hybrid service tier with an investment professional at your balance, the robo-advisor may satisfy your need for personal communication. If you want to understand automated services in depth, read our overview of [what a robo-advisor is](/guides/what-is-a-robo-advisor/).\n\nConversely, if a human professional you interview is registered exclusively as a broker-dealer representative rather than an investment adviser, the regulatory baseline shifts. Checking Form CRS confirms whether your prospective advisor operates as an investment adviser or a broker. Reviewing your personal planning targets with our [retirement calculator](/retirement/) will help clarify which advisory model supports your goals."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Our Takeaway on Picking the Right Advisory Structure",
      "content": "A robo-advisor fits a reader who wants low-cost automated investing for a defined goal and accepts little human contact, while a human adviser fits a reader who needs advice beyond investing or wants a person to call. When the human professional is a registered investment adviser, the duty of care and loyalty matches what an automated robo-adviser owes under federal law. If that professional operates as a broker or dual registrant, a different legal standard may govern transactions, as outlined in Form CRS. This assessment changes if an automated platform provides hybrid human consultation at your account balance, or if a prospective human adviser operates under a broker-only registration.\n\nTake action by examining public regulatory disclosures before opening an account with any firm. Search the firm and individual professional on the SEC [IAPD](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov) website to review their Form ADV Part 2 narrative brochure and Form CRS relationship summary. Compare total all-in costs, including advisory fees, fund expenses, and platform charges, and verify the exact scope of human communication provided in your written advisory contract."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Are robo-advisors better than financial advisors?",
      "answer": "Robo-advisors are better for investors who want automated, low-cost investment management for a defined goal and do not require personal financial planning. Human financial advisors are better for individuals with complex assets, tax planning requirements, or estate planning needs who want ongoing personal communication. The right option depends on your account size and the breadth of services you require."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is a robo-advisor a good idea?",
      "answer": "A robo-advisor is a good idea if you are seeking an automated, lower-cost way to build and manage a portfolio using exchange-traded funds. However, it may not be a good idea if you need advice across taxes and estate planning, or want a person to talk to."
    },
    {
      "question": "Robo-advisor vs financial planner: what is the difference?",
      "answer": "The regulatory sources reviewed do not provide a formal legal definition for the generic title financial planner. A robo-advisor is an automated digital investment advisory program that builds and manages securities portfolios based on questionnaire responses."
    }
  ],
